[Music]  welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Thomas Thurston who is the director  of education and public outreach at the  Gilder Lehrman Center for the Study of  slavery and abolition at the Macmillan  Center he has organized several  collaborative international institutes  for teachers in Ghana the United States  and to the UK today we'll talk with Tom  about the recent workshop series he  developed called teaching transatlantic  histories models for collaboration  between US and African teachers and  classrooms welcome Tom thanks Marilyn  it's great to be here  let's begin with an overview of the  workshops sure tell us about them well  we have ten teachers from the  Connecticut greater New Haven area  attend from Johannesburg South Africa  and ten from Sierra Leone who are  working together in teams I have 20  teachers in Connecticut half that group  is working with 10 teachers in Sierra  Leone the other half is working with 10  teachers in in South Africa part of it  involves traveling to each other's  countries so we just returned from  Johannesburg in in in June where we met  with our teachers there and we're  planning a trip to December but most of  it is building classroom to classroom  collaborative learning opportunities so  part of the reason for the travel is to  get the teachers a chance to learn more  about each other's countries histories  to get to know each other to visit  classrooms when possible all those sort  of things I am curious about how the  teachers were chosen did you can pick  them what was the process no I did I  wanted to avoid hand picking because  then I'd obviously I have my face  sorry here in Connecticut I I contact  contacted a lot of educators in the  state someone from the Department of  Education from and from the Connecticut  Council for the social studies from the  New Haven Public Schools and I asked  them to give me a group a list of  teachers who they considered their top  teachers I didn't want to do to  broadcast a kind of call for  applications because that just could  have been overwhelmed oh sure and I did  a similar thing in in in both South  Africa and in Sierra Leone where I had  people associated with school of  education at Vic's Waters rond in  Johannesburg and someone at at the  University of Sierra Leone who was like  a history educator kind of picked  teachers that they'd worked with and  that they respected and and it's all hot  to say it's been great choices all  around all the teachers are just amazing  teachers have all of the teachers been  together at one point because I the two  countries in Africa have all of the  teachers met with the teachers from the  United States just the just the group  from from a Johannesburg  we you know we went out there and had a  had a 10 day workshop out there so that  was their first opportunity to meet in  person but the group the u.s. teachers  and Sierra Leoneans they've connected  via whatsapp and so they've got to know  each other they've shared photos they've  talked about classroom projects but  December December early January will be  their first chance to kind of meet face  to face when we go and visit Sierra  Leone okay so that's later just just a  couple yeah we've kind of staggered are  the two groups just - okay that's  exciting to head back and yeah I am  curious to know how what was the impetus  behind developing this workshop series  sure well as you mentioned we did  something similar back in  inglis 2009 we began it was a three year  program with teachers from Ghana the  United States and the UK and the focus  of that three year series was on the  transatlantic slave trade but we focused  the kind of education around it by  country so we we started in Ghana and we  had teachers professors who were leading  the workshop from both the UK the United  States and Ghana who co-taught it and  then the second year we did it here at U  Young University and brought you know  brought the groups there and the third  we finished at New York in the UK and  part of them and we wanted to make sure  that this wasn't just an opportunity for  Americans or Brits to visit Africa and  to to have this great kind of experience  we wanted that to be reciprocal both and  so we made a point to make sure that  that that all that the groups were  always invited there was always a  tripartite group of teachers in the same  with the with the education we didn't  want it to be say just an American  professor just just a UK professor we  wanted to have professors who were  speaking at the workshop organizing  workshop that represented all three  countries that was important to how we  did it so that was kind of impetus and  and of course in a lot of ways is a  great success but what I felt was  lacking was anything built into the  program to make it sustainable to give  to really encourage teachers to continue  to collaborate with one another all  these were kind of site based workshops  where they a lot of learning happened  and a lot of friendships were made but  there wasn't anything happening between  classrooms and it was earlier on a  little harder to do that but also we  hadn't built it into the program and  despite that a lot of the especially  American and Ghanaian teachers they did  that anyway and to this day I last  summer I heard from a from a teacher  down in the DC area who was  going out to Ghana and he was staying  with a friend there you know that he'd  met in the workshop and they're  continuing him and others are continuing  to collaborate between classrooms so I  really wanted to make collaboration the  focus of this new series of  international workshops okay and in the  workshops the teachers are creating  curriculum what exactly is the goal of  the workshop for the teachers well part  of the goal of the workshop is to  familiarize themselves selves with the  with the various histories of the  country so in in Johannesburg we really  focused on on apartheid its origins it's  it's the end of apartheid and and  legacies and so it's both the Americans  and the teachers from Johannesburg who  were sharing this we did it at the  apartheid museum in Johannesburg so we  wanted them to get a sense of that  history so they all kind of came with  that and in Sierra Leone it's going to  be a history of the nation of Sierra  Leone but with special emphasis on the  connections with the United States in  particular here in New Haven New Haven  and Freetown Sierra Leone our sister  cities and I yeah that the Amistad  memorial that's in front of City Hall  that's those captives were Mende people  from what's now Sierra Leone and and  they know about that history probably as  much as or better than students here in  the United States sink sink a bay whose  was a leader of the captives his  portrait is at the New Haven Historical  Society he's on the 5000 Leone a note  there and everyone knows that story so I  can hold up a picture of postcard of him  that I brought from from our museum here  and asked us anyone know who this is to  a classroom of of young students they  all know I don't I hope the same would  go here in New Haven but I'm not quite  sure all right  did you notice particularly since you  have completed the workshop in  Johannesburg when the teachers from the  United States worked with the teachers  from Johannesburg were there any  differences in perspective in terms of  the history  did you know were there any surprises  that that you noticed that's an  interesting question I think what  surprised me was how engaged the  teachers in Johannesburg were with what  you would expect to be a kind of history  they already knew and they were you know  they do know that history it's it's  something that as history teachers they  that's a part of their curriculum but  there's it just reminded me that there's  always something new to hear about it we  had our professor here at Yale  University Dan Magaziner came and and so  he was able to kind of you know bring  his perspective but then we had  professors from Johannesburg and and and  former activists and and and others who  were also adding perspectives that in  some ways I think that because it  matters more to teachers in Johannesburg  they were probably more engaged in  conversation in questions than the  Americans were I think and it's not  surprising but but we as Americans it's  very easy it's understandable it's  almost what you expect to happen but we  always hear that history of apartheid  and want to relate it with Jim Crow and  civil rights movement that which is all  very well and good but you also have to  remember that that's a history in and of  itself that is not you know important  because of its semblance to what happens  here and so you're they're high school  teachers is that correct  yeah there are a mix next year we have  we have even we have even some  elementary school teachers so it's a  challenge to to work with them but it's  a mixture of teachers all with a focus  on history  okay and then moving forward what do you  hope comes out of the workshops  ultimately well what I want to see is is  is them develop classroom activities  that can be shared so the the group in  from the South Africa Connecticut group  are a little further along they've met  each other and they're already  developing curriculum most of which is  centered around either for the younger  students cultural aspects like music  dance some people are looking at  literature and sharing literature to  work and then many are talking about you  know a comparative study of the civil  rights movement here in the states and  in the anti-apartheid movement and civil  rights in fact is a subject of study in  South Africa it's important they see it  as important to understanding their  history as well so already we have some  fairly well-developed projects are going  on a number of teachers are focusing on  the role that young people played in  both the civil rights and anti-apartheid  struggle but also in contemporary and  rights-based struggle both in South  Africa and in the States so in a couple  weeks we're going to be presenting some  of our preliminary projects at the  Connecticut Council for the social  studies really yeah I'm very I'm very  impressed I was surprised that that my  Connecticut teachers were were to the  point where they said oh yeah we really  want I'd suggest it if you want you  think and they were all out of ten I  think seven of them are going to be  attending yeah I'm very emphasizing so  I'm very impressed  so the curriculum or curricula that they  have developed is that going to be  accessible to you know through the  website and everyone you know basically  in the world can access it and use it if  they'd like yeah I that that certainly  is one of the goals is it that you know  part of it is goes to this  sustainability but also how to  disseminate how to spread this so some  of it will be making resources and  curricula available online and some of  the way that we want to sustain and  disseminate is to encourage partnerships  beyond that original group so that and  usually what I imagine is is instead of  teacher to teacher thinking of as a  school to school partnership so that so  that other teachers either in social  studies or perhaps like you know English  literature or maybe even math and  science that they're interested they  they want to develop relationships so  that we have a stronger stronger  relationships in some ways there's kind  of an institutional relationship between  schools which is I think easier to  maintain than a person-to-person  relation someone moves and retires that  sort of thing okay well we will look for  those materials on the Gilder Lehrman  Center's website and the product the  project sounds very innovative and  exciting so thank you for being here  with us today and sharing it well thanks  it was my pleasure  okay for more information about Tom and  the Gilder Lehrman Center please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at you  you

